# Secrets / env
.env
*.env
kaggle.json

# Data (raw batches and DuckDB files are regenerated, not versioned)
data/raw/*.csv
data/raw_download/
# Third-party Kaggle data, downloaded on first run by
# src/model/train_churn_telco.py - not ours to redistribute, same as above.
data/raw_telco/
data/processed/
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal

# Model artifacts - blanket-ignored (training is meant to be re-run, not
# diffed), with two deliberate exceptions below.
#
# docker/Dockerfile.api does `COPY models_store /app/models_store` and the
# registry resolves whatever is newest on disk when MLflow has no
# champion alias (src/model/registry.py's documented fallback). A
# platform deploy (Render, or anyone else cloning this repo) builds from
# a fresh checkout with no local models_store/ at all - every prediction
# endpoint would 503 with "no churn model artifact found" on a perfectly
# healthy container. That is worse than a bloated repo, so the two
# artifacts actually served locally are committed rather than gitignored.
# Update these two lines (and re-run `git add -f`) after promoting a new
# champion, the same way you would update any other deployment config.
models_store/*.pkl
models_store/*.joblib
!models_store/churn_model_20260923T054451Z.joblib
!models_store/recommender_20260921T040152Z.joblib
# Metadata is cheap (a few KB of JSON each) and every version's entry
# feeds the Model Performance page's "performance across retrains" trend
# chart, so all of it is committed rather than just the two versions
# above - a deploy with only one data point on that chart would look
# broken, not minimal.
!models_store/*.json
!models_store/.gitkeep

# MLflow artifact store (docker-compose's mlflow service bind mount) -
# bookkeeping copies of the same artifacts already excluded above.
mlflow_artifacts/

# Postgres backup dumps (dags/backup_warehouse.py) - regenerated daily,
# never versioned (and would contain real customer data).
backups/*.sql.gz
!backups/.gitkeep

